{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Red Rust Bloom: Iron Snow of a Sunlit Alpine Mine Lake",
    "date": "2026-11-17",
    "lesson": "Berkeley Pit Lake, Montana, is an abandoned copper mine flooded with acidic, metal-laden groundwater (pH ~2.5). Despite the toxicity, iron-oxidizing acidophiles like Acidithiobacillus and Ferroplasma thrive by stripping electrons from dissolved ferrous iron, generating energy while precipitating rust-colored ferric particles that drift down as a strange orange marine snow. A thin photosynthetic film of acid-tolerant algae and cyanobacteria survives at the surface where light is strong but chemistry is punishing. This level asks you to play an iron-oxidizer: energy comes from a chemical plume peaking in the mid-water column rather than from digesting much of anything, so chemotaxis toward the plume matters as much as catching food. Resistance to acid stress is modeled as high baseline respiration cost and a toughened antibiotic/toxin cross-reactivity (mimicking metal/acid tolerance), while the sparse, slow-drifting particulate snow represents precipitated iron-oxide flocs rather than true organic food.",
    "citation": "Based on Berkeley Pit Lake (Butte, Montana) acidophilic iron-oxidizer ecology studies",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Berkeley Pit Lake, Butte, Montana - acidic flooded copper mine, iron-oxidizing acidophile community"
  },
